{{#> marketing}}
  {{#*inline "body-block"}}
    <div id="navigation" class="compatibility-anchor" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <section class="page-hero compare-hero">
      <div class="shell narrow-shell">
        <p class="eyebrow">Compare approaches</p>
        <h1>Compare what each library handles,<br><em>not how short the demo looks.</em></h1>
        <p class="hero-lede">Jakarta Mail, Spring Mail, Commons Email, and Simple Java Mail can all send a message. The real difference is what you still have to build, configure, secure, and maintain yourself.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="profiles" class="profiles ruled-section">
      <div class="shell">
        <header class="section-heading split-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">At a glance</p><h2>Four libraries, four different jobs</h2></div><p>Choose the one that leaves the right amount of work in your code. Direct control is valuable when you need it; a smaller feature set can be exactly enough.</p></header>
        <div class="profile-list">
          <article><span>Foundation</span><h3>Jakarta Mail / Angus Mail</h3><p>The standard MIME and mail-protocol API. You control sessions, message structure, transport use, and the conventions around them.</p><a href="https://eclipse-ee4j.github.io/angus-mail/">Official project <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article>
          <article><span>Framework integration</span><h3>Spring Mail</h3><p>Spring's sending abstraction and <code>MimeMessageHelper</code>. A natural fit for applications that mainly need Spring configuration and <code>JavaMailSender</code>.</p><a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/integration/email.html">Official documentation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article>
          <article><span>Convenience layer</span><h3>Apache Commons Email</h3><p>A smaller API for common message types. Your code supplies the validation, security, and sending setup that falls outside that API.</p><a href="https://commons.apache.org/proper/commons-email/">Official project <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article>
          <article class="profile-primary"><span>Full sending library</span><h3>Simple Java Mail</h3><p>A higher-level API for the message itself, security, shared configuration, advanced sending, diagnostics, and conversion.</p><a href="/why-simple-java-mail.html">Why Simple Java Mail <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="responsibility-matrix" class="matrix-section ruled-section">
      <div class="shell wide-shell">
        <header class="section-heading split-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Side by side</p><h2>What does each option handle for you?</h2></div><div class="matrix-legend"><span><i class="state built"></i>High-level support</span><span><i class="state helper"></i>Helper / integration</span><span><i class="state owned"></i>Your code handles it</span></div></header>
        <div class="comparison-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table">
          <table class="comparison-table">
            <thead><tr><th>What needs handling</th><th>Jakarta Mail / Angus</th><th>Spring Mail</th><th>Commons Email</th><th>Simple Java Mail</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th>Basic and rich composition</th><td data-state="owned">Direct <code>MimeMessage</code> / multipart API</td><td data-state="helper"><code>MimeMessageHelper</code></td><td data-state="helper">Typed convenience classes</td><td data-state="built">Fluent Email builder and automatic MIME structure</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Attachments, embedded content, calendars</th><td data-state="owned">You compose the body parts</td><td data-state="helper">Helper for common MIME parts</td><td data-state="helper">Common attachments and HTML</td><td data-state="built">Built-in attachments, inline content, pre-encoding, iCalendar</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Recipient policy and validation</th><td data-state="owned">You build this</td><td data-state="owned">You add it around the sender/helper</td><td data-state="helper">Basic recipient setters</td><td data-state="built">Builders, parsing, validation, defaults, forced overrides</td></tr>
              <tr><th>DKIM signing</th><td data-state="owned">You add an extension or integration</td><td data-state="owned">You add an integration</td><td data-state="owned">You add an integration</td><td data-state="built">Optional module; per-message or mailer default</td></tr>
              <tr><th>S/MIME signing and encryption</th><td data-state="owned">You add an extension or integration</td><td data-state="owned">You add an integration</td><td data-state="owned">You add an integration</td><td data-state="built">Optional module; send, read, decrypt, per-recipient certificates</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Transport policy and OAuth2</th><td data-state="owned">Session properties and authenticators</td><td data-state="helper">Spring configuration plus Jakarta properties</td><td data-state="helper">Common server and TLS settings</td><td data-state="built">Named transport strategies, OAuth2, verification, raw-property escape hatch</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Async results and connection tests</th><td data-state="owned">You build this</td><td data-state="owned">You or your framework builds this</td><td data-state="owned">You build this</td><td data-state="built">Built-in async result and sync/async connection testing</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Connection reuse and bounded batches</th><td data-state="owned">You open, reuse, and close the transport</td><td data-state="owned">You build this</td><td data-state="owned">You build this</td><td data-state="built">Simple batch and callback-scoped open connection</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Pooled and multi-cluster delivery</th><td data-state="owned">You build this infrastructure</td><td data-state="owned">You build this infrastructure</td><td data-state="owned">You build this infrastructure</td><td data-state="built">Optional pooled batch module with keyed clusters</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Authenticated SOCKS</th><td data-state="owned">You add the network integration</td><td data-state="owned">You add the network integration</td><td data-state="owned">You add the network integration</td><td data-state="built">Optional authenticated SOCKS bridge module</td></tr>
              <tr><th>EML, MimeMessage, and Outlook conversion</th><td data-state="helper"><code>MimeMessage</code> is the native model</td><td data-state="helper">Direct access to <code>MimeMessage</code></td><td data-state="owned">You add conversion support</td><td data-state="built">Email/MimeMessage/EML plus optional Outlook MSG conversion</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Java, properties, and Spring configuration</th><td data-state="helper">Session properties / Java</td><td data-state="built">Native Spring configuration</td><td data-state="helper">Java convenience configuration</td><td data-state="built">Builders, property layers, environment, and Spring module</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Diagnostics and settings inspection</th><td data-state="helper">Protocol debug stream and direct objects</td><td data-state="helper">Framework logging plus Jakarta tools</td><td data-state="helper">Underlying exceptions and debug tools</td><td data-state="built">Connection tests, inspectable config, debug routing, validation, receipts</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Low-level escape hatch</th><td data-state="built">The API itself</td><td data-state="built">Direct Jakarta objects and properties</td><td data-state="helper">Underlying JavaMail/Jakarta integration</td><td data-state="built">Custom Session, properties, MimeMessage, and send operation</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="matrix-note">“Your code handles it” does not mean the feature is impossible. It means you build it yourself or add another integration. If a cell is inaccurate, <a href="{{site.issues}}">please report it</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="scenarios" class="scenario-section ruled-section">
      <div class="shell">
        <header class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Start with your project</p><h2>What do you actually need?</h2></header>
        <div class="scenario-list">
          <article><span>01</span><div><h3>I need full low-level control</h3><p>Choose Jakarta Mail / Angus directly when you want to build and manage sessions, transports, and MIME parts yourself.</p></div></article>
          <article><span>02</span><div><h3>I need Spring's standard sending integration</h3><p><a href="/configuration.html#section-spring-support">Simple Java Mail integrates directly with Spring</a> through its Spring module, property configuration, and an injectable mailer. If your requirements stop at <code>JavaMailSender</code> and <code>MimeMessageHelper</code>, Spring Mail itself may already be sufficient.</p></div></article>
          <article><span>03</span><div><h3>I need a small convenience layer</h3><p>Commons Email may be enough when you need a shorter API for common messages and are happy to handle validation, security, and delivery setup elsewhere.</p></div></article>
          <article class="scenario-primary"><span>04</span><div><h3>I need the API to keep up as requirements grow</h3><p>Simple Java Mail keeps the easy send easy, then adds security, shared rules, advanced delivery, diagnostics, and conversion without making you switch libraries or drop down to Jakarta Mail.</p><a href="/download.html">Try the first send <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="scope" class="scope-band">
      <div class="shell scope-grid"><p class="eyebrow">Scope matters</p><h2>Simple Java Mail focuses on outbound application email.</h2><p>Jakarta Mail also covers protocols such as IMAP and POP3 and remains the right choice for mailbox access. Simple Java Mail builds on it to compose, secure, and send messages.</p></div>
    </section>
  {{/inline}}
{{/marketing}}
